ord of ids, any argument, and any `Command`, `Call`, `ArgValue`, `Outcome`, `Refusal` or `Fault`: an argument fielded by its schema name, or a `Command` or an `Outcome` fielded with `Debug`, would print an id, typed text, an accessible name or a control's value (of the argument names only `text` is in the sink's scrub set, §8); the driver emits no event and installs no subscriber, so with none installed the span is disabled, under escher's sink it is one line per call at `info` or below (§6) and at the default `warn` it is filtered out before it is created; not driven: no stand step reads not settled, so `busy` is covered by its unit test only. Since 2026-10-10-driver-cli the span is also written by a hosted call: the `escher-session` host role decodes a wire `call` and runs it through `Session::run` and nothing else, so each hosted call is one closed `command` span under that binary's sink — measured, 3 lines for 3 calls at `RUST_LOG=trace` (`host_log`); a session-level verb (`start` · `status` · `stop`) or, since 2026-10-12-self-description, the tool-level `help` handed to `Session::run` is refused `unknown-verb` and its span still records the verb's table word; a call the schema refuses on the command line or in the client's `call` is answered there, reaches no session and opens no span; the span keeps its eight fields. Still observed absent — a span, an event or a `tracing` dependency in the settle wait itself, `Harness::settle` in `packages/blitz-test-harness`, and a span, an event or a line of their own on `Session::start`, `Session::act`, `serve`, `start`, `attach`, `stop`, the wire — its `call` request and reply framing included — the client's `call`, `command_line` and its argv reader — its `help` ending, since 2026-10-12-self-description, reaching no session and opening no span — `describe()`, the `start` command's host spawn, and the host's idle expiry, which like a `stop` writes no record — and since 2026-10-10-mcp-surface `serve_mcp` itself and the rest